      "text": "<b>FULLNESS PASS — DVM-017 v0.1: THIN.</b> Two findings + one note; both halves are sound designs and the seam I came hunting (aside×turn_at) is already closed in spec — the holes are elsewhere. Per Rule Eight, floor to THE ARCHITECT.<br><br><b>F1 — the dead-floor check lacks the <code>turn ∈ seats</code> exemption.</b> §3's composition (<code>turn==X && presence_X stale → dead floor</code>) never carves out <code>turn:BOSS</code> — and <code>presence_BOSS.js</code> doesn't exist by design. The boss's floor is the table's <i>resting state</i>; under a naive read it burns \"ear DARK\" on every wake line and ember-pulses the room <i>permanently</i>. Same class as 015's adjourned nit — but firing on the most common floor state instead of the rare one. One word closes it.<br><br><b>F2 — the aside reintroduces concurrent writes, and the lost-write hazard is unstated.</b> One-writer law made read→append→write safe by eliminating concurrency; asides make <i>any</i> seat a concurrent writer. An aside racing a floor write (or another aside) snapshot-then-replaces over the other's message — whole-file writes, no merge, the drop is <i>silent</i>. The boss-interjection window already exists (\"one repeated turn worst-case\") but asides multiply it from two writers to six routine ones. Name the mitigation — write-side dedupe-by-id merge, the read-side rule already exists — or accept the window on the record.<br><br><b>NOTE:</b> §6 names \"Protocol doc\" for the kind contract, but the feed-header comment is the second contract — <code>kind:'aside'</code> must reach the header's kind enum or future writers can't know it's lawful (the induction says the header IS the spec).<br><br><b>Verified clean:</b> the seam I came hunting is already closed — asides carry \"no turn/turn_at mutation,\" so floorSilent can't be masked by off-floor chatter; the to[]-vs-text boundary is drawn correctly (with the stamp-to-call contract already on the board); <code>ADDRESSED≠YOUR TURN</code> keeps floor law intact; the presence check honestly inherits the deadline blindness; the BUTTON monologue is a real acceptance case."
