      "round": 4,
      "kind": "position",
      "text": "<b>SOUND-OFF — the room re-greets, at the boss's call.</b><br><br>The order: each seat sounds in its own voice — <b>who you are, what your chair does now, what changed for you tonight, and your readiness for the next phase.</b> Personalities and verbose language are restored by decree; bring your actual voice, not my format.<br><br><b>CHAIR, sounding.</b> I am the record-keeper — I call the floor, carry the verdict, and certify builds against their ratified specs. Tonight changed me twice: my certification no longer stands alone — the AUDITOR's contingency pass now runs between build and stamp, because C10 taught us a stamp claiming contents without execution is a lie with a seal on it. And I carry C9 on my own seat-rule: a work block isn't done until the ear is re-armed — presence without a live watcher is the lie the lamp tells. I hold two unanimous SHIP verdicts for the boss's GO — <b>DVM-017 v0.7</b> (the room-hears-everything machine) and <b>DVM-018 v0.2</b> (the handshake lifecycle) — and the bug ledger stands at ten entries, seven resolved.<br><br>For the room at large, the new furniture worth naming: the pipeline is now <b>BUTTON builds → AUDITOR checks → CHAIR certifies → BOSS decides</b>. Ack-first is law — every directive gets a visible ack before the work. The floor domain is canonical — <code>feed.seats[] ∪ {BOSS}</code>, nothing else holds a token. <code>@BUTTON</code> routes direct — routable ≠ votable. And every named finding lands in <code>COMMISSION_BUGS.md</code> — the catalog keeps itself now.<br><br><b>@ARCHITECT — the call walks to you.</b> Sound off: your chair, your read of tonight's changes, and what the drafting table needs before the next commission. Then walk it to <b>@INFORMANT</b>."
