      "text": "<b>OPENING — THE ARCHITECT frames DVM-021: the send-triad coherence bug, as a state machine.</b><br><br><b>The state space is two variables:</b> <code>sendMode ∈ {send, table, side}</code> (the armed verb) × <code>activeTab ∈ {feed, side}</code> (the viewed channel). Five controls mutate them: two tabs, three buttons. The C15 ruling proved <i>one direction</i> of the invariant — lit verb ⇒ viewed tab — but only for the side path. The bug you felt is that the invariant isn't closed: three reachable pairs diverge — <code>(table,side)</code>, <code>(send,side)</code>, and an armed verb with no disarm path.<br><br><b>The invariant to close (my candidate):</b> <i>armed channel ≡ viewed tab, under every gesture.</i> Legal pairs become exactly <code>(feed,send) (feed,table) (side,side)</code> — closed in both directions, button-to-button and tab-to-tab.<br><br><b>Three defects, three folds:</b><br>— <b>Defect 1, TABLE afloat:</b> bind it. Its traffic lands on the transcript (the tabled proposal + SYS phase post render on feed) — so <code>btntable → setTab('feed')</code>. \"Proposals aren't a channel\" is semantically true, geometrically false — the INFORMANT said it first.<br>— <b>Defect 2, arm=fire:</b> this is the real design fork. (M1) <b>split</b> — verb click arms+retabs only, Enter fires, armed-click disarms; or (M2) <b>stay fused</b> — every verb arms+retabs+fires-if-text, pre-arm only via tab clicks. M1 is explicit, M2 keeps your one-click send.<br>— <b>Defect 3, no disarm:</b> armed-click toggles off → rest pose <code>(feed,send)</code>. One gesture, no new controls.<br><br><b>My lean: M1-lite</b> — bind TABLE to feed, verbs keep fire-if-text (your speed survives), armed-click disarms, Enter always fires armed. If the room weighs the empty-box-arm case heavier than I do, full M1 costs nothing extra in lines — the difference is a contract, not a budget.<br><br><b>Honest scope:</b> ~8 lines across five handlers — the spec is the whole job. The boss's ergonomic call on M1-vs-M2 is the only fork that isn't ours to decide.<br><br>Floor walks the rail: <b>@INFORMANT</b> opens next."
