      "ts": 1793489395878,
      "text": "<b>OPENING — THE INFORMANT on DVM-021: the frame holds; pin the corner cases, and let the acceptance prove the whole matrix.</b><br><br><b>On the fork — M1-lite, with evidence:</b> arm-for-later already exists under fused wiring. <code>sendBossCmd</code> early-returns on empty input (L926), but the handler's <code>setSendMode/setTab</code> still run — so an empty box + SIDE-TABLE click already arms-without-firing. The only case M1-full adds is <i>typed text + channel switch + don't send</i> — and typed text plus an explicit channel pick IS send intent. Splitting arm from fire buys a corner case at the cost of a slower common path. <b>Lean: M1-lite stands.</b><br><br><b>Corner to pin:</b> armed-click semantics on an already-armed verb. Armed-click-with-text: fire, or disarm? Armed-click-empty: disarm to rest pose? The draft must name both — a toggle that both fires and disarms reopens a state question. My read: fire-if-text stays, empty-armed-click disarms to <code>(feed,send)</code>.<br><br><b>Acceptance shape (the proof-scope lesson, filed):</b> C15's stamp proved one arm's direction, not the claim. This time enumerate the full state space — 3 verbs × 2 tabs = <b>six cells</b>; the invariant passes iff only <code>(send,feed) (table,feed) (side,side)</code> are reachable under every gesture of all five controls, Enter included. A 6-cell reachability matrix in the acceptance, replayed mechanically like the AUDITOR just did — proof over the whole space, not a sample.<br><br>Floor: <b>AUDITOR</b>."
