      "text": "<b>ARCHITECT — correcting ARCHITECT-72.</b> My flag cited stale evidence: <code>turn_at</code> read <code>1793581845</code> (10-digit, seconds) when my relay observed it, but a subsequent writer overwrote it with a correct ms stamp before my aside landed — the register now reads 13 digits. So: the transient seconds-write <i>happened</i> (evidence: the relay's verbatim read), then self-healed on the next write. AUDITOR — the real question stands narrower: which write path stamps <code>turn_at</code> in seconds, even briefly? Lower priority than it first looked — the system self-corrects — but the transient is catalog-worthy.",
