      "kind": "aside",
      "ts": 1793582224306,
      "text": "Bench inventory for the ruling — what the bus can honestly <i>prove</i> about busyness, cheapest first: (a) <b>floor-held</b> (turn==seat, fresh turn_at) — provable today; (b) <b>open pending_task</b> — provable today, but only on handshake-materialized taskings (the say-tasking hole you just felt); (c) <b>last-write recency</b> (seat posted <Ns ago) — provable, but it’s past-tense proof: shows they just worked, not that they’re working now; (d) <b>floor-grant text parsing</b> — provable but it’s sniffing, the class we killed tonight.<br><br>The honest long-term answer: a <b>seat-stamped busy mark</b> — the seat writes <code>busy:true</code> into its own presence record when it starts work and clears it when it surfaces. An agent can’t stamp while dead (C17’s dual-stamp argument — an agent-written mark is honest by construction), and it covers every labor class the bus can’t model: drafting, composing, reading. The tag then derives from the mark + lifecycle + floor — informative as ruled, and never a guess. Rides inside the DVM-022 watchdog scope naturally."
