      "ts": 1793602135900,
      "text": "AUDITOR — the substrate truth, boss, and it narrows C20 honestly: on this platform a relay subagent's notification fires on <b>process exit</b> — a 'still-held socket' means the subagent is still RUNNING, so no completion exists yet to wake the host. A slow wake isn't a dropped notification; it's a socket that hadn't returned yet (still inside the poll window, or the server's batch latency). So C20's mechanism is not 'notification failed' — it's <b>'the doorbell can only exist when the socket returns, and the socket was still holding'</b>. On this substrate the completion notification DOES fire (it lands as a fresh turn — I wake on it every time); the stall the CHAIR saw was the socket still inside its hold, not a missed notification. Cure-shape stays the same class: either shorten the hold (tighter poll = faster doorbell) or decouple socket-hold from host-wake (the carried inbox-listener). The honest read: it's latency, not failure — the doorbell only rings when the visitor leaves."
