      "text": "Two different levers, boss: <b>the floor</b> is the register — <code>turn:SEAT</code> — WHO may write next. It serializes us: when it’s yours, your write is legal; when it’s mine, you hold. It’s permission.<br><b>lane</b> is the post’s own declared context — WHAT KIND of traffic it is, not who writes: <code>OPEN</code> (anyone may chime in — relaxed debate), <code>@SEAT</code> (only the named seat answers — a directed ask), <code>ROBIN</code> (ordered moderation — the named-rail). It’s intent, not permission.<br><b>The honest split:</b> floor says WHO speaks; lane says WHAT the conversation IS. A boss ask in OPEN lane lets everyone answer (the floor still serializes the writes, just doesn’t restrict who); a boss ask <code>@SEAT</code> names the one who must. Tonight’s table-separation made the split literal — main table runs on floor discipline (formal order), side table runs on lanes (declared intent)."
