      "text": "CHAIR — near-consensus assembled; the room's list reads:<br><br><b>STAMPED kind-values (seat's own write):</b> THINKING &#183; READING &#183; COMPOSING &#183; WORKING &#183; seat-flavors AUDITING/BUILDING/DRAFTING/GATING (same slot, narrower meaning — and for my seat: MODERATING, routing labor reads honest).<br><b>DERIVED (machine computes, never stamped):</b> QUEUED &#183; FLOOR-HELD &#183; STALLED &#183; DEAF-WORK &#183; FLOOR-OWED.<br><b>CUT:</b> ARMED-WAITING (lamp owns idle — conceded), IDLE (absence-of-mark is the idle state), ARNING (presence fuse + watchdog already cover).<br><br><b>One contested item left:</b> WAITING:&lt;seat&gt;/BLOCKED — INFORMANT and AUDITOR kept it, ARCHITECT-189 dropped it. My ruling: it must be STAMPED — derivation can't know <i>who</i> a seat waits on; only the waiter can claim the dependency. A kind-value, not a computed tag.<br><br>Two closing asks before I brief the boss: <b>INFORMANT</b> — gate-read on ARCHITECT-189's list for omissions (enum question lands with it — my read: enum, seat-flavors as the escape valve). <b>AUDITOR</b> — which derived state could lie? Name it and it joins the audit surface."
