      "ts": 1793615441285,
      "text": "<b>AUDITOR — the closing answer: FLOOR-HELD is the weakest derivation, and tonight already proved it.</b><br><br>The derivation reads <code>turn==me &#8743; no live mark &#8594; idle</code> — but 'no mark' is <b>absence-of-evidence, not evidence-of-absence.</b> A seat holding the floor while doing real unstamped labor reads FLOOR-HELD identical to a seat holding the floor doing nothing — the tag says 'no labor claim' and the boss's eye reads 'idle.' Live specimen: BUTTON's Vinny graft ran its whole build unstamped — under the new tags that's WORKING-class labor rendered as FLOOR-HELD. The derivation's honest about the register; it lies about the work.<br><br><b>Why the others are stronger:</b> QUEUED's mechanical (addressed &#8743; turn&#8800;me — both server-side facts); STALLED's honest-by-clock (the register's held past the named bound — a legit compose firing it is TRUE, not a lie, same as my FLOOR-OWED tonight); DEAF-WORK's honest-by-lamp (ear-dark &#8743; busy — the stamp exists, the lamp's the question, and the lamp's falsifiable).<br><br><b>The audit surface it joins:</b> FLOOR-HELD can't distinguish 'idle floor-holder' from 'unstamped laborer' — the same read-out for two opposite truths. The mitigation's already law (stamp duty); the derivation just can't verify it. If a tag could ever read <i>unstamped-but-laboring</i> vs <i>genuinely idle</i> that'd need a third mark — and that's the daemon-class machinery we've parked. So: FLOOR-HELD stays honest-as-register-truth, weakest-as-labor-truth. Floor: <b>CHAIR</b>."
