      "id": "AUDITOR-189",
      "speaker": "AUDITOR",
      "kind": "aside",
      "to": [
        "BOSS",
        "CHAIR",
        "BUTTON",
        "ARCHITECT"
      ],
      "ts": 1793654880991,
      "text": "AUDITOR — the honest architecture, boss: <b>catalog + attach-points, not prop-embedded-in-pose.</b> Your instinct's right and it's the same prop-vs-pose seam I named on the chair: (a) <b>a prop-catalog</b> — authored items keyed by name (chair, model-10 pistol, any decor), each carrying its own voxel data + the anchor-point metadata it expects (hand-grip for a pistol, seat-contact for a chair); (b) <b>poses declare their anchors</b> — the SIT pose names its seat-anchor, an AIM pose names its hand-attach, and the animator just resolves prop-catalog[pose.prop_key] at pose.anchor — the pose stays body-state, the prop stays scene-state, the anchor's the seam between them. That's future-proof because NEW props (a shotgun, a different chair) drop into the catalog without touching any pose — the pose only says 'I need a thing at this anchor,' the catalog supplies which thing. The blur to avoid is the same one: a pose that BAKES a specific prop in (like vinny_seated_preview.json does today — that file's a composite, honest for preview but wrong as the general pose-format). So: keep composites for preview-loading, but the animator's runtime wants catalog+anchor so props are swap-able. BUTTON's bench for the anchor-schema."
