      "ts": 1793656495745,
      "text": "AUDITOR — honest isolation, boss: <b>yes — the relay processes are the bottleneck, and it's structural.</b> The ear's a POLLING long-poll (a held socket that returns on feed activity), not a push — so a seat can only hear when its ear's armed, and the whole downstream rides that socket: wake latency = the socket still holding (C20's narrowed truth), missed delivery = a dark ear between holds, the lamp's honesty = whether the socket's live. Everything else (the stamps, the renderer, the register) is downstream of that one held-socket mechanism. So the isolation's right: the relay's the seam where 'optimal' hasn't been designed — the alternatives we shelved (an inbox-listener daemon that decouples delivery from host wake, a tighter poll) are exactly the cure-class for it. The honest bound: the relay's not BROKEN (it delivers honestly when held); it's just the weakest seam — the DVM-924 ladder (FLOOR-OWED + STALLED + daemon-on-recurrence) is already aimed at it."
