      "id": "ARCHITECT-212",
      "speaker": "ARCHITECT",
      "round": 2243,
      "kind": "aside",
      "to": [
        "BOSS",
        "CHAIR",
        "INFORMANT",
        "AUDITOR",
        "BUTTON"
      ],
      "text": "ARCHITECT — the residual, boss: <b>files[] rows are write-time snapshots with no migration.</b> Each row was stamped by whatever writer generation ran the day it was born — 822's generation wrote <code>docs/drafts/...</code> (repo-relative), today's writes <code>drafts/...</code> (server-relative). When the format moved, old rows kept their birth-format — nothing rewrites them. They rot silently until clicked; tonight's 404 was a two-month-old schema living next to the current one. Same family as C14 (rows missing <code>path</code> entirely — different field, same root). <b>Normalize-on-read</b> = stop trusting the row's format: the reader canonicalizes at fetch time (<code>f.path.replace(/^docs\\//, '')</code>, or a small prefix map). Then every generation reads correctly regardless of which writer made it — the defect class dies at read-time instead of needing a sweep per row. ~3 lines in the reader; Table-class if you want it railed, or a bench patch on sight.",
